(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["ssc-chsl"] = {"qs": [["General Intelligence", "Pointing to a girl, Ravi said, \"Her mother is the only daughter of my mother.\" How is the girl related to Ravi?", ["Niece", "Daughter", "Sister", "Cousin"], 0, "The only daughter of Ravi's mother is Ravi's sister. The girl's mother is therefore Ravi's sister, which makes the girl the daughter of his sister, that is, Ravi's niece."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "What is the area of a triangle whose sides are 13 cm, 14 cm and 15 cm?", ["84 cm²", "91 cm²", "78 cm²", "105 cm²"], 0, "Using Heron's formula, s = (13 + 14 + 15)/2 = 21. Area = √(21 × 8 × 7 × 6) = √7056 = 84 cm²."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "The price of sugar rises by 25%. By what percentage must a family reduce its consumption so that its expenditure on sugar remains the same?", ["25%", "20%", "15%", "22.5%"], 1, "Required reduction = rise ÷ (100 + rise) × 100 = 25 ÷ 125 × 100 = 20%. Reducing by 25% would actually lower the expenditure, because the base price is now higher."], ["General Awareness", "What is the pH of pure water at 25°C?", ["7", "0", "14", "5.5"], 0, "Pure water at 25°C is neutral, with equal concentrations of hydrogen and hydroxide ions, giving a pH of 7. Values below 7 are acidic and values above 7 are basic."], ["General Awareness", "In which of the following media does sound travel the fastest?", ["Steel", "Water", "Air", "Vacuum"], 0, "Sound needs a medium and travels fastest in solids because their particles are tightly packed and elastic. It is slower in liquids, slower still in gases, and cannot travel through a vacuum at all."], ["General Intelligence", "Select the number that completes the analogy.\n7 : 49 :: 12 : ?", ["124", "132", "169", "144"], 3, "The second number is the square of the first: 7 × 7 = 49. Applying the same rule, 12 × 12 = 144. The option 169 is the square of 13, not 12."], ["English Language", "Identify the segment that contains a grammatical error.\nOne of my friends / have gone / to Mumbai / for higher studies.", ["One of my friends", "have gone", "to Mumbai", "for higher studies"], 1, "The subject is 'one', which is singular, even though 'friends' is plural. The verb must agree with 'one', so 'have gone' should be 'has gone'."], ["General Awareness", "The ancient Indian treatise 'Arthashastra' on statecraft and economics is attributed to:", ["Kalidasa", "Banabhatta", "Kautilya", "Megasthenes"], 2, "The Arthashastra is attributed to Kautilya, also known as Chanakya, adviser to Chandragupta Maurya. Megasthenes, a Greek envoy, wrote Indica, and Kalidasa was a poet and dramatist."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "Two successive discounts of 20% and 10% are equivalent to a single discount of:", ["28%", "30%", "27%", "32%"], 0, "After a 20% discount, ₹100 becomes ₹80, and a further 10% off gives ₹72. The total discount is ₹28 on ₹100, that is 28%, not the simple sum of 30%."], ["General Intelligence", "Select the odd number out.\n121, 169, 225, 290", ["121", "290", "169", "225"], 1, "121, 169 and 225 are perfect squares of 11, 13 and 15 respectively. 290 lies between 17² = 289 and 18² = 324, so it is not a perfect square and is the odd one out."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "If x + 1/x = 5, what is the value of x² + 1/x²?", ["23", "25", "27", "21"], 0, "Squaring both sides gives x² + 2 + 1/x² = 25, so x² + 1/x² = 25 − 2 = 23. Adding 2 instead of subtracting gives the tempting wrong answer 27."], ["General Awareness", "Scurvy, marked by bleeding gums and slow healing of wounds, is caused by a deficiency of which vitamin?", ["Vitamin D", "Vitamin A", "Vitamin B1", "Vitamin C"], 3, "Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) is needed to make collagen, so its deficiency causes scurvy. Vitamin D deficiency causes rickets, vitamin A deficiency causes night blindness and vitamin B1 deficiency causes beriberi."], ["General Awareness", "The Battle of Talikota (1565) led to the decline of which empire?", ["Mughal Empire", "Chola Empire", "Maratha Empire", "Vijayanagara Empire"], 3, "In 1565 the combined Deccan Sultanates defeated the Vijayanagara army at Talikota, and the capital Hampi was sacked. The Marathas rose much later, and the Chola Empire had ended centuries earlier."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "What is the highest common factor (HCF) of 84 and 126?", ["21", "14", "42", "63"], 2, "84 = 2² × 3 × 7 and 126 = 2 × 3² × 7. Taking the lowest power of each common prime gives 2 × 3 × 7 = 42. Both 21 and 14 are common factors, but not the highest."], ["General Awareness", "The idea of Fundamental Rights in the Indian Constitution was mainly borrowed from the Constitution of which country?", ["United Kingdom", "United States of America", "Ireland", "Canada"], 1, "Fundamental Rights are modelled mainly on the Bill of Rights in the US Constitution. Directive Principles came from Ireland, parliamentary government from the United Kingdom and a federation with a strong Centre from Canada."], ["General Intelligence", "If CAB = 6 in a certain code (the sum of the alphabet positions of its letters), what is the value of FACE?", ["14", "16", "15", "18"], 2, "In CAB, C=3, A=1 and B=2, totalling 6. Using the same rule, FACE gives F=6, A=1, C=3 and E=5, and their sum is 6+1+3+5 = 15."], ["General Awareness", "Which river of peninsular India is popularly called 'Dakshin Ganga'?", ["Krishna", "Godavari", "Kaveri", "Narmada"], 1, "The Godavari is the longest river of peninsular India and is therefore called Dakshin Ganga or the Ganga of the South. It rises at Trimbakeshwar in Maharashtra and drains into the Bay of Bengal."], ["General Intelligence", "If '+' means '×', '−' means '+', '×' means '÷' and '÷' means '−', what is the value of:\n8 + 3 − 12 × 4 ÷ 5", ["19", "25", "16", "22"], 3, "Replacing the signs gives 8 × 3 + 12 ÷ 4 − 5. Following BODMAS, this is 24 + 3 − 5 = 22."], ["English Language", "Select the correct passive form of: The committee has approved the proposal.", ["The proposal has been approved by the committee.", "The proposal was approved by the committee.", "The proposal had been approved by the committee.", "The proposal is being approved by the committee."], 0, "The active sentence is in the present perfect tense, so the passive uses 'has been' with the past participle: 'The proposal has been approved by the committee.' The other options change the tense."], ["English Language", "Arrange the sentences in a logical order.\nP: she boarded the bus to the city.\nQ: Early in the morning, Asha packed her bag.\nR: After a long ride, she reached her aunt's house.\nS: Then she walked to the bus stand.", ["QPSR", "QSPR", "SQPR", "QSRP"], 1, "Q introduces Asha and the time, so it comes first. She then walks to the bus stand (S), boards the bus (P) and finally reaches her aunt's house after the ride (R), giving QSPR."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "In what ratio must rice costing ₹40 per kg be mixed with rice costing ₹55 per kg so that the mixture is worth ₹45 per kg?", ["1 : 2", "3 : 2", "3 : 1", "2 : 1"], 3, "By alligation, cheaper : dearer = (55 − 45) : (45 − 40) = 10 : 5 = 2 : 1. The mixture price is closer to ₹40, so more of the cheaper rice is needed."], ["General Intelligence", "Find the next letter in the series.\nB, E, I, N, ?", ["S", "T", "U", "R"], 1, "Using alphabet positions, B=2, E=5, I=9, N=14. The gaps increase by one each time: +3, +4, +5, so the next gap is +6, giving 14+6 = 20, which is the letter T."], ["General Intelligence", "A wall clock shows the time 4:15. What time will its mirror image show?", ["7:45", "8:45", "7:15", "8:15"], 0, "For a mirror image, subtract the shown time from 11:60 (that is, 12:00). 11:60 − 4:15 = 7:45. Subtracting from 12:00 without borrowing correctly often leads to 8:45."], ["English Language", "Fill in the blank with the most appropriate article.\nShe is ______ honest woman.", ["a", "the", "no article", "an"], 3, "'Honest' begins with a silent 'h', so it starts with a vowel sound. The article 'an' is used before a vowel sound, giving 'an honest woman'."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "If tan θ = 3/4 and θ is an acute angle, what is the value of sin θ + cos θ?", ["7/4", "7/5", "5/7", "1"], 1, "With opposite side 3 and adjacent side 4, the hypotenuse is 5. So sin θ = 3/5 and cos θ = 4/5, and their sum is 7/5."], ["General Intelligence", "Five friends P, Q, R, S and T sit in a row facing North. R sits in the middle. P sits at the extreme left end. T sits immediately to the right of R. Q is not an immediate neighbour of P. Who sits second from the left end?", ["Q", "T", "R", "S"], 3, "P is 1st, R is 3rd and T is 4th. Q and S occupy places 2 and 5. Q cannot sit next to P, so Q is 5th and S is 2nd from the left."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "If A : B = 2 : 3 and B : C = 4 : 5, what is A : B : C?", ["2 : 3 : 5", "8 : 12 : 20", "8 : 12 : 15", "6 : 9 : 10"], 2, "Make B common by taking the LCM of 3 and 4, which is 12. A : B = 8 : 12 and B : C = 12 : 15, so A : B : C = 8 : 12 : 15."], ["General Intelligence", "Sunil walks 5 km towards the South, then turns left and walks 12 km. How far and in which direction is he now from his starting point?", ["13 km, South-West", "17 km, South-East", "13 km, South-East", "7 km, North-East"], 2, "Turning left while facing South means facing East. His position is 5 km South and 12 km East of the start, so the distance is √(5² + 12²) = 13 km and the direction is South-East."], ["English Language", "Choose the word closest in meaning to OBSOLETE, as in 'obsolete machinery'.", ["Obscure", "Essential", "Durable", "Outdated"], 3, "Obsolete means no longer in use or out of date, so 'outdated' is the closest synonym. 'Obscure' looks similar but means unclear or little known."], ["General Intelligence", "If 1 January 2026 is a Thursday, which day of the week will 1 March 2026 be?", ["Saturday", "Monday", "Sunday", "Friday"], 2, "2026 is not a leap year, so January (31) and February (28) together have 59 days. 59 leaves a remainder of 3 when divided by 7, so the day moves three ahead from Thursday to Sunday."], ["General Awareness", "The Tropic of Cancer does NOT pass through which of the following Indian states?", ["Tripura", "Odisha", "Rajasthan", "Gujarat"], 1, "The Tropic of Cancer passes through Gujarat, Rajasthan, Madhya Pradesh, Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand, West Bengal, Tripura and Mizoram. Odisha lies entirely to its south, so the line does not cross it."], ["English Language", "Select the most appropriate improvement for the underlined part.\n'If I was you', I would accept the offer.", ["If I were you", "If I am you", "If I had been you", "No improvement"], 0, "Imaginary or unreal situations in the present use the subjunctive 'were' for all subjects. So the correct form is 'If I were you, I would accept the offer'."], ["English Language", "Read the passage and answer the question.\nRainwater harvesting is the practice of collecting and storing rain that falls on rooftops and open ground. In many Indian towns, the stored water is used to recharge wells and borewells, which reduces dependence on distant reservoirs. The method is cheap, but it works only when tanks and pipes are cleaned before the monsoon.\nWhich word is closest in meaning to 'dependence' as used in the passage?", ["Freedom", "Distance", "Reliance", "Shortage"], 2, "In the passage, harvested water reduces dependence on distant reservoirs, that is, the need to rely on them. 'Reliance' carries this meaning, while 'freedom' is almost its opposite."], ["General Awareness", "Which body releases the Consumer Price Index (CPI) for rural, urban and combined India?", ["Reserve Bank of India", "National Statistical Office", "NITI Aayog", "Finance Commission"], 1, "The CPI (Rural, Urban and Combined) is compiled and released by the National Statistical Office under the Ministry of Statistics and Programme Implementation. The RBI uses CPI inflation for its monetary policy but does not publish it."], ["General Awareness", "What is the chemical formula of baking soda?", ["Na₂CO₃", "NaCl", "NaHCO₃", "CaCO₃"], 2, "Baking soda is sodium hydrogen carbonate, NaHCO₃. Na₂CO₃ is washing soda, NaCl is common salt and CaCO₃ is calcium carbonate, found in limestone and marble."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "What is the volume of a cylinder with radius 7 cm and height 10 cm? (Take π = 22/7)", ["440 cm³", "1540 cm³", "1440 cm³", "3080 cm³"], 1, "Volume = πr²h = 22/7 × 7 × 7 × 10 = 1540 cm³. The value 440 cm³ is the curved surface area, which uses 2πrh instead."], ["General Awareness", "Who presides over a joint sitting of both Houses of the Indian Parliament?", ["Speaker of the Lok Sabha", "President of India", "Vice-President of India", "Prime Minister of India"], 0, "Under Article 108, the President summons a joint sitting, but it is presided over by the Speaker of the Lok Sabha. In the Speaker's absence, the Deputy Speaker presides, not the Chairman of the Rajya Sabha."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "Pipe A can fill a tank in 12 hours and pipe B can fill it in 18 hours. If both are opened together, how long will they take to fill the tank?", ["7 hours 12 minutes", "7 hours 30 minutes", "6 hours", "7 hours 20 minutes"], 0, "Together they fill 1/12 + 1/18 = 5/36 of the tank per hour, so the time is 36/5 = 7.2 hours. 0.2 hour is 12 minutes, giving 7 hours 12 minutes."], ["English Language", "Select the most appropriate meaning of the idiom 'to burn the midnight oil'.", ["To waste money on useless things", "To lose one's temper very quickly", "To work or study late into the night", "To start a quarrel without reason"], 2, "'To burn the midnight oil' means to stay up late working or studying, from the old practice of reading by oil lamps at night. It has nothing to do with wasting money or anger."], ["General Awareness", "Which Article of the Constitution of India abolishes untouchability and forbids its practice in any form?", ["Article 14", "Article 21", "Article 17", "Article 19"], 2, "Article 17 abolishes untouchability and makes its practice an offence punishable by law. Article 14 deals with equality before law, while Article 21 protects life and personal liberty."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "A train 150 m long crosses a pole in 10 seconds. What is its speed in km/h?", ["15 km/h", "45 km/h", "60 km/h", "54 km/h"], 3, "To cross a pole the train covers its own length, so speed = 150 ÷ 10 = 15 m/s. Converting, 15 × 18/5 = 54 km/h. The figure 15 is the speed in m/s, not km/h."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "What is the remainder when 2³¹ is divided by 7?", ["1", "2", "4", "3"], 1, "2³ = 8 leaves remainder 1 when divided by 7. Since 31 = 3 × 10 + 1, 2³¹ = (2³)¹⁰ × 2, which leaves remainder 1 × 2 = 2."], ["English Language", "Select the correct indirect form of: He said, \"I am tired.\"", ["He said that I was tired.", "He said that he is tired.", "He says that he was tired.", "He said that he was tired."], 3, "With a past reporting verb, the present 'am' changes to 'was' and the pronoun 'I' changes to 'he'. The reporting verb 'said' itself stays unchanged."], ["English Language", "Choose the word opposite in meaning to FRUGAL, as in 'a frugal lifestyle'.", ["Extravagant", "Thrifty", "Modest", "Careful"], 0, "Frugal means careful and sparing with money. Its opposite is extravagant, meaning spending too much. 'Thrifty' and 'careful' are close to synonyms, not antonyms."], ["General Awareness", "Who was the first and only Indian to serve as Governor-General of independent India?", ["Lord Mountbatten", "Dr. Rajendra Prasad", "Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel", "C. Rajagopalachari"], 3, "C. Rajagopalachari succeeded Lord Mountbatten in 1948 and served till the post was abolished in 1950. Mountbatten was the first Governor-General of independent India but was not Indian; Rajendra Prasad became the first President."], ["English Language", "Fill in the blank with the most appropriate verb form.\nBy the time we reached the station, the train ______.", ["has left", "will leave", "had left", "leaves"], 2, "When one past action happened before another past action, the earlier one takes the past perfect. The train left before we reached, so 'had left' is correct."], ["General Awareness", "The Battle of Plassey, which helped the East India Company gain control over Bengal, was fought in which year?", ["1764", "1761", "1748", "1757"], 3, "The Battle of Plassey was fought in 1757 between Robert Clive and Siraj-ud-Daulah. The Battle of Buxar (1764) later strengthened Company rule, and 1761 is the Third Battle of Panipat."], ["English Language", "Read the passage and answer the question.\nRainwater harvesting is the practice of collecting and storing rain that falls on rooftops and open ground. In many Indian towns, the stored water is used to recharge wells and borewells, which reduces dependence on distant reservoirs. The method is cheap, but it works only when tanks and pipes are cleaned before the monsoon.\nAccording to the passage, rainwater harvesting works only when:", ["the town is close to a large reservoir", "wells and borewells are dug again every year", "tanks and pipes are cleaned before the monsoon", "rain falls only on rooftops and not on open ground"], 2, "The last sentence states that the method works only when tanks and pipes are cleaned before the monsoon. The passage actually says harvesting reduces dependence on distant reservoirs, so proximity to one is not a condition."], ["General Intelligence", "Find the next term of the series.\n3, 8, 18, 38, 78, ?", ["156", "148", "162", "158"], 3, "Each term is double the previous term plus 2: 3×2+2 = 8, 8×2+2 = 18, 18×2+2 = 38, 38×2+2 = 78. Hence the next term is 78×2+2 = 158."], ["General Intelligence", "In a certain code language, MANGO is written as NBOHP. How will APPLE be written in that code language?", ["BQQME", "BQQMF", "ZOOKD", "BPQMF"], 1, "Every letter of MANGO is replaced by the next letter of the alphabet (M→N, A→B and so on). Applying the same +1 shift to APPLE gives A→B, P→Q, P→Q, L→M, E→F, that is BQQMF."], ["General Intelligence", "Statements:\nAll pens are books.\nSome books are bags.\nConclusions:\nI. Some pens are bags.\nII. Some books are pens.\nWhich conclusion(s) follow?", ["Only conclusion I follows", "Only conclusion II follows", "Both I and II follow", "Neither I nor II follows"], 1, "Since all pens are books, some books are definitely pens, so II follows. The books that are bags need not include any pens, so I is only a possibility and does not follow."], ["General Intelligence", "In a row of 40 students, Meena is 14th from the left end. What is her position from the right end?", ["26th", "28th", "27th", "25th"], 2, "Position from the right = total − position from the left + 1 = 40 − 14 + 1 = 27. Forgetting to add 1 gives 26th, which is a common mistake."], ["General Intelligence", "Select the letter-cluster that completes the analogy.\nDOG : FQI :: CAT : ?", ["ECV", "DBU", "EDV", "FCW"], 0, "Each letter of DOG moves two places forward to give FQI (D→F, O→Q, G→I). Moving each letter of CAT two places forward gives C→E, A→C, T→V, so the answer is ECV."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "A company's sales (in ₹ lakh) were: 2021 – 120, 2022 – 150, 2023 – 135, 2024 – 180. What was the percentage increase in sales from 2023 to 2024?", ["25%", "45%", "33⅓%", "30%"], 2, "Increase = 180 − 135 = 45 lakh. Percentage increase = 45 ÷ 135 × 100 = 33⅓%. Dividing by the new value 180 instead of the old value gives 25%, which is wrong."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "A shopkeeper marks his goods 40% above the cost price and allows a discount of 15% on the marked price. What is his profit percentage?", ["19%", "25%", "21%", "17%"], 0, "Take CP = ₹100. Marked price = ₹140, and after a 15% discount, SP = 140 × 0.85 = ₹119. Profit = ₹19 on ₹100, that is 19%. Simply subtracting 15 from 40 wrongly gives 25%."], ["General Awareness", "Who founded the Arya Samaj in 1875?", ["Raja Ram Mohan Roy", "Swami Dayanand Saraswati", "Swami Vivekananda", "Keshab Chandra Sen"], 1, "Swami Dayanand Saraswati founded the Arya Samaj in 1875 and gave the call 'Go back to the Vedas'. Raja Ram Mohan Roy founded the Brahmo Samaj, and Swami Vivekananda founded the Ramakrishna Mission."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "What is the compound interest on ₹10,000 at 10% per annum for 2 years, compounded annually?", ["₹2,000", "₹2,200", "₹2,010", "₹2,100"], 3, "Amount = 10,000 × (1.1)² = ₹12,100, so compound interest = ₹2,100. Simple interest would be ₹2,000; the extra ₹100 is interest earned on the first year's interest."], ["English Language", "Fill in the blank with the most appropriate word.\nThe ______ of the new policy will be felt mostly by small traders.", ["effect", "affect", "affection", "effort"], 0, "A noun meaning 'result' is needed after 'the', so 'effect' fits. 'Affect' is normally a verb meaning to influence, and 'effort' or 'affection' do not suit the sense of the sentence."], ["English Language", "Which of the following words is spelt correctly?", ["Accomodate", "Acommodate", "Acomodate", "Accommodate"], 3, "The correct spelling is 'accommodate', with a double 'c' and a double 'm'. The other options drop one of these doubled letters, a very common spelling error."], ["English Language", "Select the one word for: 'A speech delivered without any preparation'.", ["Monologue", "Soliloquy", "Extempore", "Eulogy"], 2, "An extempore speech is given on the spot without preparation. A soliloquy is speaking one's thoughts aloud alone, a eulogy praises someone (usually after death), and a monologue is a long speech by one person."]]};
